In this paper, we study the stability threshold of the two-dimensional incompressible MHD equations near Couette flow and a constant horizontal magnetic field \(( eta,0)\) on \(\mathbb T	imes\mathbb R\). For \(N>10\), \(0<
u,\mu\le1\) and \(| eta|\ge(
u+\mu)/(3\sqrt{
u\mu})\), we prove global stability for initial perturbations of size \(\delta_N\min\{
u,\mu\}^{1/3}\) in \(H^N\), where \(\delta_N>0\) is independent of \(
u,\mu, eta\). We also obtain enhanced dissipation of the nonzero horizontal modes on the time scale \(\min\{
u,\mu\}^{-1/3}\), together with inviscid damping of the vertical components. The proof combines time-dependent Fourier multipliers with an energy correction constructed using the Alfvén oscillations. By combining the nonlinear terms arising from this correction, we obtain the cancellation needed to close the energy estimates at the $1/3$ scale.
